The ride-hailing scenario is finished and passes all three delivery conditions. All 5 difficulties (2 tasks each) passed the policy audit and the knowledge-base audit. The whole-scenario load check (`validate_scenario`) returns VALID with no warnings. I re-ran every gate myself at the end, and all are green.

The scenario is in `env/env_0056_ride_hailing_platform/`, for a fictional company called Wayfare Mobility.

| Difficulty | Product | Policy audit | KB audit |
|---|---|---|---|
| rider_trip_fares_cancellations_and_refunds | trip | PASS (round 3) | PASS (round 3) |
| pass_membership_billing_and_benefits | pass | PASS (round 4) | PASS (round 2) |
| cleaning_and_damage_fee_assessments | damage | PASS (round 3) | PASS (round 2) |
| driver_earnings_bonuses_and_payouts | earnings | PASS (round 1) | PASS (round 1) |
| driver_account_holds_and_deactivations | driver | PASS (round 3) | PASS (round 2) |

There are 358 knowledge-base documents across the 5 products above plus two that no difficulty uses: driver car rentals (16 docs) and bikes and scooters (18). Those two only go through the automatic document check, never an audit, as the process intends. No contact detail or name is shared between difficulties.

Things you should know:

- **I edited the frozen blueprint once.** The identity-check tool in `blueprint/share_tools.py` counted matches in a form the solvability gate can't read, so that gate failed for every difficulty. Nothing could fix this inside a single difficulty, so I rewrote the count in an equivalent form. I confirmed every tool's name, parameters and description are unchanged and the results are identical. No difficulty had been audited yet, so nothing that had passed was undone. The decision is in the worklog.
- **Two problems only showed up once other products' documents were published.**
    - **Trip and pass:** a Wayfare Pass rule made an extra step owed after the trip task's fare correction, and the credit would have been recovered twice. The trip side was changed to avoid this, then re-audited and its documents rebuilt.
    - **Damage:** some fee factors were defined in terms of other factors. That was fixed in the damage policies and went through the same loop.
- **Some imperfect wording in the frozen blueprint was kept as written.** It is in the rental, bike, pass, driver and damage parameter descriptions; for example, a few descriptions still state rules that newer policies replaced. None of it changes any task's answer. Each item is logged in `worklog.md` as `BLUEPRINT-KNOWN`.
- **Some realism trade-offs remain**, logged by the auditors as observations, not failures. A few computation factors are policies the instantiators invented rather than standard industry practice. Driver ID fields on some riders' trips are left empty, and the driver's earnings ledger is not updated when a damage fee is charged.

`.audit_views/` is kept inside the scenario folder because the audit reports cite its file hashes; the scenario loader ignores it. `worklog.md` has a line for every launch, round and result. I also saved the solvability-gate and cross-product lessons to memory for future runs.
